\newlabel{ch:c19}{{21}{1276}{Merge-Sort and Persistent Trees}{chapter.21}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.0.1}{\ignorespaces The merge-sort tree over \(A\): the \emph  {same} heap intervals as Ch.19's sum tree (Figure~\ref {fig:c13-tree}), but each node now stores its interval's \textbf  {\textcolor {deepblue}{sorted list}} instead of a single aggregate. Only the top three levels are drawn; a deeper node's list is a contiguous run of the list above it, and the leaves \(t_{16..31}\) are the single elements. The two green nodes \(t_4,t_5\) are the ones the running count query touches. Build merges lists on the way up (\(\ensuremath  {O(n\log n)}\)); counting values \(\le x\) over a range bisections each of the \(O(\log n)\) canonical nodes (\(\ensuremath  {O(\log ^2 n)}\)).}}{1278}{figure.caption.230}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c19-mst}{{21.0.1}{1278}{The merge-sort tree over \(A\): the \emph {same} heap intervals as Ch.19's sum tree (\figref {c13-tree}), but each node now stores its interval's \term {sorted list} instead of a single aggregate. Only the top three levels are drawn; a deeper node's list is a contiguous run of the list above it, and the leaves \(t_{16..31}\) are the single elements. The two green nodes \(t_4,t_5\) are the ones the running count query touches. Build merges lists on the way up (\(\bigO {n\log n}\)); counting values \(\le x\) over a range bisections each of the \(O(\log n)\) canonical nodes (\(\bigO {\log ^2 n}\))}{figure.caption.230}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows~1--2 are the two static ideas (a merge-sort tree counts, path copying preserves); row~3 is the memory law that makes persistence actually run; row~4 marries persistence to the value axis (prefix versions); row~5 is the payoff -- \(k\)-th by joint descent, extended to tree paths by four roots; row~6 is the \(O(1)\) static idempotent special case, the sparse table. Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: SPOJ~KQUERY on row~1, CF~707D on rows~2--3, SPOJ MKTHNUM on rows~4--5, SPOJ~COT on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~1 and~4 (a \(k\)-th-in-a-range the merge-sort tree samples and the chairman computes deterministically).}}{1280}{figure.caption.231}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c19-taxonomy}{{21.0.2}{1280}{The chapter's six-state taxonomy, read top to bottom as an escalation. Rows~1--2 are the two static ideas (a merge-sort tree counts, path copying preserves); row~3 is the memory law that makes persistence actually run; row~4 marries persistence to the value axis (prefix versions); row~5 is the payoff -- \(k\)-th by joint descent, extended to tree paths by four roots; row~6 is the \(O(1)\) static idempotent special case, the sparse table. Every solved problem sits on exactly one row: SPOJ~KQUERY on row~1, CF~707D on rows~2--3, SPOJ MKTHNUM on rows~4--5, SPOJ~COT on row~5, and the Summit spanning rows~1 and~4 (a \(k\)-th-in-a-range the merge-sort tree samples and the chairman computes deterministically)}{figure.caption.231}{}}
